    "title": "The City That Learned To Watch",
    "essay": "The City That Learned To Watch\n\nThe city had learned to watch without looking. At first the system was almost invisible. A camera here. A database there. A forgotten identifier. Nobody noticed because nothing appeared to change. The streets looked exactly as they had the year before. The buildings kept their doors. The people kept their routes.\n\nFragments\n\nThen the fragments began to connect. A camera became a record. A record became a pattern. A pattern became a prediction about a person who had not done anything yet. The connections were not visible from the street. They were visible only from inside the system, where the city had stopped being a place and started being a graph. And eventually the city itself seemed to know where everyone was.\n\nOrdinary Decisions\n\nThe strange thing was that nobody had decided to build a machine that watched everyone. It emerged from thousands of ordinary decisions. A camera installed for safety. A database created for convenience. A number assigned because numbers were easier than names. A form that asked for an address. A contract that ran for seven years. Each decision made sense by itself. Nobody signed off on the whole. Together they created something else.\n\nIn Plain Sight\n\nNothing was hidden. The cameras were on poles in plain sight. The registers were published. The contracts were public documents that anyone could read. Fourteen departments held the same seven fields. The city was not concealing the machine. It had simply never described it as one.\n\nThe Same Street\n\nAnd the street is still a street. The buildings have not moved. The people walk the same routes to the same doors. The light falls the way it always fell. Only the meaning of the walk has changed.",
